# hugmesh_authkeys.fc
# Label the device-registry AuthorizedKeysCommand binary as the entrypoint
# of hugmesh_authkeys_t, and the signed-export cache it reads as
# hugmesh_authkeys_cache_t (sshd_t->hugmesh_authkeys_t domtrans + the
# nobody-domain read in the .te then apply). provision_selinux()'s
# first-boot relabel (setfiles / restorecon -RF /) applies these.
#
# The binary is copied to /usr/sbin by the (protected) build script; the
# signed export + baked public key + node.env live under
# /etc/hugmesh/registry (staged by provision_device_registry + written
# by the sync agent).

/usr/sbin/hugmesh-authorized-keys-lookup    --    gen_context(system_u:object_r:hugmesh_authkeys_exec_t,s0)

/etc/hugmesh/registry(/.*)?                       gen_context(system_u:object_r:hugmesh_authkeys_cache_t,s0)

################################################################
# FOLDED-IN: hugmesh_breakglass.fc (was a LABEL-ONLY .fc with no .te)
#
# docs/selinux-module-loading.md's step (b) note: a .fc with no .te has
# nothing for the refpolicy devel Makefile to compile into a standalone .pp,
# so it would ship as a SILENTLY MISSING label. Of the two clean options the
# doc gives, option (1) — fold the lines into a sibling module that DOES have
# a .te — is the recommended one, and is what happened here; no stub .te was
# invented. hugmesh_authkeys is the login-CREDENTIAL module (it already owns
# the sshd AuthorizedKeysCommand domain and its cache), so both folded
# contributions are credential-file labels consumed by a login path.
# The doc names this exact fold: "add the break-glass line to hugmesh_authkeys.fc".
# The original file's documentation is preserved verbatim below.
#
# NOTE: these lines only carry FILE CONTEXTS. They add no type, no domain and
# no allow rule to hugmesh_authkeys_t — the referenced types (ssh_home_t,
# user_runtime_t) are base refpolicy types, resolved when semodule builds the
# store. scripts/_selinux-policy-lib.sh enforces the .fc<->.te pairing so a
# future label-only file cannot be dropped silently again.
################################################################
# Give the console/loopback-only break-glass authorized_keys file an
# sshd-readable authorized-keys context. break-glass.conf re-opens
# `AuthorizedKeysFile /etc/ssh/break-glass.keys` for the loopback Match
# (specs/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/break-glass.conf); a file created at runtime
# by a physically-present operator there would otherwise be etc_t, which
# sshd_t does NOT read as an authorized_keys source under enforcing — so
# the break-glass recovery would silently fail closed (no recovery path).
#
# ssh_home_t is the standard refpolicy type for ~/.ssh/authorized_keys and
# is what sshd_t is permitted to read as a key source. Labelling the
# break-glass file ssh_home_t makes the loopback recovery work on enforcing
# without widening sshd. The file is ABSENT/empty by default (no bypass);
# this only governs the LABEL it gets once an operator drops a key in.
# provision_selinux()'s first-boot relabel applies this; a runtime-created
# file needs `restorecon /etc/ssh/break-glass.keys` after it is written
# (document in the break-glass runbook).
#
# registry-verify.sh (specs/usr/lib/hugmesh/registry-verify.sh) is a
# helper invoked from the AuthorizedKeysCommand / break-glass path; it runs
# in the caller's domain (hugmesh_authkeys_t) and needs no separate
# entrypoint label, so no .te domain is added for it here — confine it only
# if the AVC pass shows it transitioning.

/etc/ssh/break-glass\.keys    --    gen_context(system_u:object_r:ssh_home_t,s0)

################################################################
# FOLDED-IN: hugmesh_oidc_token.fc (was a LABEL-ONLY .fc with no .te)
#
# docs/selinux-module-loading.md's step (b) note: a .fc with no .te has
# nothing for the refpolicy devel Makefile to compile into a standalone .pp,
# so it would ship as a SILENTLY MISSING label. Of the two clean options the
# doc gives, option (1) — fold the lines into a sibling module that DOES have
# a .te — is the recommended one, and is what happened here; no stub .te was
# invented. hugmesh_authkeys is the login-CREDENTIAL module (it already owns
# the sshd AuthorizedKeysCommand domain and its cache), so both folded
# contributions are credential-file labels consumed by a login path.
# The doc's preferred home is "the desktop-session module", which does not exist yet; until the fleet adds a confined desktop domain this is the closest real sibling. Moving it there later is a one-line move.
# The original file's documentation is preserved verbatim below.
#
# NOTE: these lines only carry FILE CONTEXTS. They add no type, no domain and
# no allow rule to hugmesh_authkeys_t — the referenced types (ssh_home_t,
# user_runtime_t) are base refpolicy types, resolved when semodule builds the
# store. scripts/_selinux-policy-lib.sh enforces the .fc<->.te pairing so a
# future label-only file cannot be dropped silently again.
################################################################
# Label the per-user OIDC-token deposit directory that the SSO desktop-login
# integration writes (the bearer-forward GEN-SSO artifact reads it). The token
# lands at ${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/hugmesh/oidc-token == /run/user/%{uid}/hugmesh/
# oidc-token, user-private 0600, wiped on logout
# (specs/etc/hugmesh/desktop-governance/discover/bearer-forward.sh.tmpl reads
# it and configures the PER-USER flatpak http.extra-headers Bearer).
#
# WHY this label: /run/user/%{uid} is the user runtime dir (refpolicy
# user_runtime_t under systemd / user_tmp_t historically). A hugmesh subdir
# created at session start under it would otherwise inherit the parent's
# context, which is usually fine — but pinning it to the desktop user runtime
# type makes the contract explicit so the SSO depositor (the writer) and
# bearer-forward.sh (the reader, running in the user's desktop/login session
# domain) agree on the label, and a future confined desktop domain can be given
# a single narrow rule against this type instead of all of user_runtime_t.
# user_runtime_t is the modern systemd /run/user/UID type; if the target's
# refpolicy predates it, swap to user_tmp_t (the .te-less label-only change).
#
# IMPORTANT (PROTECTED INSTALL HOOK — DO NOT EDIT THOSE FILES): the
# bearer-forward template + its session-start/SSO-deposit install hook land on
# the PROTECTED specs-x86/ + clients/ desktop images (build-x86-host-stage4.sh /
# the client image leg), which are FLEET-OWNED. This .fc file lives in the
# EDITABLE specs/selinux/ so it ships with the rest of the node-policy modules;
# wiring WHERE it is compiled/installed is the same fleet build-script change
# described in docs/selinux-module-loading.md. Nothing here edits the protected
# desktop artifacts — it only declares the label their runtime path must carry.
#
# This is a label-only contribution: there is NO hugmesh_oidc_token.te.
# user_runtime_t is a base refpolicy type (no module declares it here), so this
# .fc must be compiled into a module that `require {}`s it OR (preferred) folded
# into the desktop-session module when the fleet adds a confined desktop domain.
# As a standalone .fc it is applied by provision_selinux()'s restorecon; the
# 0600 file's own restorecon at session start keeps a runtime-created path
# correctly labelled (document in the desktop-session runbook).
#
# NOTE: %{uid} is the refpolicy file_contexts per-user expansion; setfiles /
# restorecon expand it per real uid at relabel time.

/run/user/%{uid}/hugmesh(/.*)?    gen_context(system_u:object_r:user_runtime_t,s0)
